# The app-identity conformance scene (docs/app-identity-plan.md): an app
# says what it is called and what it looks like, and the platform shows
# both.
#
# WHAT THIS SCENE EXISTS TO CATCH is the silent fallback, one tier up
# from the typeface's. Every platform draws SOMETHING where an app's
# icon goes — a generic executable, the host process's mark, a
# monochrome placeholder — so a declaration that never reached the
# platform, bytes the decoder refused, and a working identity are
# indistinguishable to any observation that reads back what kaya stored.
# `expect_app_icon` reads the four quadrant CENTRES off the picture the
# platform ended up holding, and that read is the whole point of the
# scene: the declaration echoed back would pass while nothing was drawn.
#
# WHY A VENDORED MARK. The expected string has to be one string on every
# lane AND unreachable by any platform's own default, and no picture can
# be both unless this tree makes it. The guests therefore declare the
# VENDORED mark's bytes (guests/assets/icons/kaya-mark.png, four flat
# quadrants), the blob rides the same channel the typeface's font does,
# and four unmistakable colours are what a per-platform conversion can
# preserve where a hash of the converted bytes could not.
# KAYA_ICON_FILE is how a runner that cannot see the repo points at a
# pushed copy — the typeface's variable, one asset over.
#
# THE READ IS OF THE ARTIFACT, NOT OF THE MODEL. On Windows that means
# WM_GETICON on the real HWND — the picture the taskbar and alt-tab draw
# from, answered out of the window manager's own state — decoded to
# pixels. A read of what kaya stored, or of the TitleBar's IconSource
# property, is an echo: sixteen zero bytes would answer it identically.
#
# No opening settle: the first expect's bounded retry IS the
# scene-ready wait.
expect label#0 "identity"
expect_app_icon "E01B24/33D17A/1C71D8/F6D32D"
# THE PRIMARY WINDOW PROMOTES ONE COMMAND, and this line is why the
# scene can be trusted to have exercised the SECOND Windows sink. A
# custom caption is minted by a promotion and by nothing else, and a
# custom caption replaces the system one — taking the system-drawn app
# icon with it — so `TitleBar.IconSource` is what puts the mark back.
# Reading that property would be a pure ECHO and is refused
# (docs/app-identity-plan.md I8); what this line CAN say is that the
# promotion happened, so the arm was reached. The pixels themselves are
# evidence a capture carries, not a scene.
expect_toolbar
expect_toolbar_item "Save" "done"
# THE NAME IS THE OTHER HALF OF THE DECLARATION, and it lands where
# every platform puts an app's name: on a window that has none of its
# own. A declared name never OVERRIDES a window's own title — the window
# below says what it is and keeps saying it — it fills the blank, which
# is what the caption, the taskbar tooltip and the alt-tab label all
# read from one string on Windows.
expect_title "identity"
expect_title window#1 "Aurora Notes"
# AN IDENTITY NEVER CHANGES WHAT ANYTHING DOES. It is chrome: the field
# still takes text and the button still fires its handler — the roles'
# contract one tier over, and why this scene carries live widgets rather
# than one label.
set_text entry#0 "hi"
click button#0
expect label#1 "clicked hi"
# AND THE MARK IS STILL THERE AFTERWARDS. Identity is declared once,
# before the first mount, and nothing an app does later may disturb it:
# a lowering that re-applied on some later pass, or a window that took
# the icon away when it took the focus, fails here and nowhere else.
expect_app_icon "E01B24/33D17A/1C71D8/F6D32D"
